feat(skills): spend the prompt's skill budget on the closest skills

The block lists skills in the order the registry happens to hold them, which is
`BTreeMap` order — alphabetical. Discovery searches roots by precedence, the
workspace root last so that its skills shadow user-wide ones, and a name sort
throws that away.

On this machine the cost is exact: `~/.agents/skills` holds twenty-five AWS
skills whose names sort early, the workspace has one, and the alphabetical
order gives the budget to the set nobody asked for. The workspace's own skill is
the one that does not fit.

`Skill` now remembers which root it came from and where that root sits in the
precedence order, and `skills_by_precedence` sorts by that, by name within a
root. The prompt is built from it. The alphabetical view is kept for the
listings a client reads, where a stable order is what matters.

The test is the shape of the problem: two user-wide skills that sort before one
workspace skill, asserting the workspace one comes first and that the listing
still reads alphabetically.

This is not relevance ordering — nothing here knows which skills a session has
used. It is the cheaper half, and it needs no state: the directory layout
already says which skills are closest to the work.
